{"id":"P2-22.1","sourceCase":"22.1","sourceLecture":27,"part2":true,"kind":"DDx","title":"Original source case Part 2 · 22.1","source":"Cases_Part2.docx · Case 22.1 · approved source","signal":"8 y intact female Rottweiler","history":"PU/PD for 2 weeks, lethargy, reduced appetite; mucopurulent vulvar discharge for 3 days; last heat 8 weeks ago","exam":"T 39.9 C, HR 130, tacky mucous membranes (about 5% dehydrated), painful caudal abdomen","panels":[{"name":"Selected source results · full report below","rows":[["WBC (x10^3/µL)","34.2","6.0-17.0"],["Band neutrophils","3.1","0-0.3"],["HCT (%)","34","37-55"],["BUN (mg/dL)","14","8-28"]]},{"name":"Additional selected source results","rows":[["Calcium (mg/dL)","10.4","9.5-11.5"],["ALT (U/L)","48","10-100"],["Urine dipstick","pH 6.5, protein","pH 6.0-7.5"]]}],"smear":"blood smear with toxic neutrophils and a left shift; urine sediment inactive (0-2 WBC and 0-1 RBC per HPF, no casts, no bacteria)","photos":["Part 2 source image placeholder — see original case instructions"],"questions":[],"differentials":[{"name":"Pyometra-associated nephrogenic diabetes insipidus","tier":"best","support":"hyposthenuria with normal BUN and creatinine = tubular function intact but ADH-resistant; pyometra is the most likely cause, confirm with abdominal imaging. The patient is dehydrated yet still dilute, which makes the finding stronger","against":"Use the original source comparison rather than assuming the diagnosis from one result.","test":"Review the source document's rule-in/rule-out clues and recommended next tests."},{"name":"Hypercalcemia","tier":"possible","support":"See approved source evidence comparison (revealed after submission).","against":"Use the original source comparison rather than assuming the diagnosis from one result.","test":"Review the source document's rule-in/rule-out clues and recommended next tests."},{"name":"Hypokalemia","tier":"possible","support":"See approved source evidence comparison (revealed after submission).","against":"Use the original source comparison rather than assuming the diagnosis from one result.","test":"Review the source document's rule-in/rule-out clues and recommended next tests."},{"name":"Central diabetes insipidus","tier":"unlikely","support":"See approved source evidence comparison (revealed after submission).","against":"Use the original source comparison rather than assuming the diagnosis from one result.","test":"Review the source document's rule-in/rule-out clues and recommended next tests."},{"name":"Primary renal failure","tier":"unlikely","support":"See approved source evidence comparison (revealed after submission).","against":"Use the original source comparison rather than assuming the diagnosis from one result.","test":"Review the source document's rule-in/rule-out clues and recommended next tests."}],"originalReport":"Case 22.1 · DDx · Dog\nSource: Urinalysis 2026, USG and hyposthenuria (slides 10, 12; red text in the 2025 deck);\npatient data invented\nSignalment / History: 8 y intact female Rottweiler; PU/PD for 2 weeks, lethargy, reduced\nappetite; mucopurulent vulvar discharge for 3 days; last heat 8 weeks ago\nExam: T 39.9 C, HR 130, tacky mucous membranes (about 5% dehydrated), painful caudal\nabdomen\nLab data\n\nTest                 Patient               Reference            Flag\nWBC (x10^3/µL)       34.2                  6.0-17.0             ↑\nBand neutrophils     3.1                   0-0.3                ↑\n(x10^3/µL)\nHCT (%)              34                    37-55                ↓\nBUN (mg/dL)          14                    8-28\n\nTest                Patient              Reference            Flag\nCreatinine          0.8                  0.5-1.4\n(mg/dL)\nCalcium (mg/dL)     10.4                 9.5-11.5\nPotassium           4.2                  3.6-5.3\n(mmol/L)\nALT (U/L)           48                   10-100\nUrine specific      1.005                ≥1.030 (dog,         ↓\ngravity                                  adequate)\n(refractometer,\ncystocentesis)\nUrine dipstick      pH 6.5, protein      pH 6.0-7.5\n                    neg, glucose neg,\n                    blood neg\n\nCytology / smear: blood smear with toxic neutrophils and a left shift; urine sediment\ninactive (0-2 WBC and 0-1 RBC per HPF, no casts, no bacteria)\n📷bloodPhoto to add: refractometer / USG category table (Urinalysis 2026 slides 10 and 12) plus a\n        smear with toxic neutrophils (no slide in this lecture; use a leukogram-lecture image)\nYour task: List your top 3-5 differentials for this USG and the next test for each before you\nscroll.\nMy differentials:","originalReview":"Answer\n\nDifferential                             Rule in / rule out clue\nPyometra (endotoxin makes tubules      Intact bitch in diestrus, purulent\nunresponsive to ADH = nephrogenic      discharge, fever, neutrophilia with\nDI)                                    left shift, USG 1.005\nHypercalcemia (nephrogenic DI)         Ruled out: Ca 10.4 is normal\nHypokalemia (nephrogenic DI)           Ruled out: K 4.2 is normal\nLiver failure                          Unlikely: ALT, BUN normal; confirm\n                                       with bile acids (Added)\nCentral DI (ADH deficiency)            Diagnosis of exclusion once the above\n                                       are ruled out; desmopressin response\n                                       trial (Added)\nRenal failure                          Ruled out: USG <1.008 is\n                                       hyposthenuria, tubules are still\n                                       actively diluting; no azotemia\n  •    Final interpretation: hyposthenuria with normal BUN and creatinine = tubular\n       function intact but ADH-resistant; pyometra is the most likely cause, confirm with\n\n       abdominal imaging. The patient is dehydrated yet still dilute, which makes the finding\n       stronger\n  •    Concept link: hyposthenuria (<1.008) = tubules diluting (nephrogenic or central DI);\n       isosthenuria (1.008-1.013) = tubules can neither concentrate nor dilute (slide 12)\nMy notes:"},
{"id":"P2-22.2","sourceCase":"22.2","sourceLecture":27,"part2":true,"kind":"SBA","title":"Original source case Part 2 · 22.2","source":"Cases_Part2.docx · Case 22.2 · approved source","signal":"6 y MN Golden Retriever","history":"PU/PD, weight loss and swelling of the ventral abdomen and hind legs for 3 weeks; no drugs","exam":"BCS 3/9, ventral edema, mild ascites (fluid wave), systolic BP 175 mmHg","panels":[{"name":"Selected source results · full report below","rows":[["HCT (%)","31","37-55"],["BUN (mg/dL)","58","8-28"],["Calcium (mg/dL)","9.8","9.5-11.5"],["Albumin (g/dL)","1.3","2.8-3.9"]]},{"name":"Additional selected source results","rows":[["Globulin (g/dL)","3.1","2.0-4.0"]]}],"smear":"urine sediment: 0-1 RBC and 0-2 WBC per HPF, no bacteria, 2-3 granular casts per LPF","photos":["Part 2 source image placeholder — see original case instructions"],"questions":[{"q":"Which interpretation best explains the urine protein result?","options":["Pre-renal (overflow) proteinuria from hypertension","Glomerular protein-losing nephropathy with nephrotic syndrome","Tubular proteinuria from acute proximal tubular injury","Proteinuria from urinary tract inflammation or hemorrhage","Bence-Jones proteinuria from multiple myeloma"],"answer":1,"why":"Answer: B UPC 5.8 (profound, ≥2.0) plus hypoalbuminemia, hypercholesterolemia and ascites/edema = nephrotic syndrome from a glomerulopathy."}],"differentials":[],"originalReport":"Case 22.2 · SBA · Dog\nSource: Urinalysis 2026, isosthenuria, proteinuria mechanisms, nephrotic syndrome, casts and\nUPC (slides 10, 12, 16, 17, 33, 48, 49); patient data invented\nSignalment / History: 6 y MN Golden Retriever; PU/PD, weight loss and swelling of the\nventral abdomen and hind legs for 3 weeks; no drugs\nExam: BCS 3/9, ventral edema, mild ascites (fluid wave), systolic BP 175 mmHg\nLab data\n\nTest                Patient             Reference           Flag\nHCT (%)             31                  37-55               ↓\nBUN (mg/dL)         58                  8-28                ↑\nCreatinine          3.3                 0.5-1.4             ↑\n(mg/dL)\nPhosphorus          7.6                 2.5-6.0             ↑\n(mg/dL)\nCalcium (mg/dL)     9.8                 9.5-11.5\nAlbumin (g/dL)      1.3                 2.8-3.9             ↓\nGlobulin (g/dL)     3.1                 2.0-4.0\nCholesterol         612                 110-320             ↑\n(mg/dL)\nUrine specific      1.012               ≥1.030 (dog,        ↓\ngravity                                 adequate)\n(refractometer)\nUrine protein       3+                  neg to 1+ if USG    ↑\n(dipstick)                              adequate\nUrine               5.8                 <0.5                ↑\nprotein:creatinin\ne ratio (UPC)\nUrine pH, blood,    6.5, neg, neg       pH 6.0-7.5\nglucose\n\nCytology / smear: urine sediment: 0-1 RBC and 0-2 WBC per HPF, no bacteria, 2-3 granular\ncasts per LPF\n\n📷(slidePhoto\n         33)\n             to add: nephrotic syndrome graphic (Urinalysis 2026 slide 17) and a granular cast\n\n\nQuestion: Which interpretation best explains the urine protein result?\nA. Pre-renal (overflow) proteinuria from hypertension\nB. Glomerular protein-losing nephropathy with nephrotic syndrome\nC. Tubular proteinuria from acute proximal tubular injury\nD. Proteinuria from urinary tract inflammation or hemorrhage\nE. Bence-Jones proteinuria from multiple myeloma","originalReview":"Answer: B UPC 5.8 (profound, ≥2.0) plus hypoalbuminemia, hypercholesterolemia and\nascites/edema = nephrotic syndrome from a glomerulopathy.\n  •    A: pre-renal/overflow proteinuria is mild and does not cause hypoalbuminemia and\n       edema; BP 175 is a consequence, not the explanation\n  •    C: tubular proteinuria is rarely above a mild (1 to 2+) result\n  •    D: no RBC, WBC or bacteria in the sediment; inflammation and UTI must be ruled out\n       before interpreting a UPC\n  •    E: the strip detects albumin, not light chains (needs immunoelectrophoresis, SSA does\n       not separate them); globulins and calcium are normal\nConcept link: nephrotic syndrome = proteinuria + hypoalbuminemia + ascites/edema +\nhypercholesterolemia; azotemia with USG 1.012 = isosthenuria (tubules lose concentrating\nability at about 2/3 loss); marked proteinuria can overestimate USG (slide 12). Added: the\nlecture lists an azotemic UPC cutoff of >0.4; >0.5 is the usual cutoff\nMy notes:"},
{"id":"P2-22.3","sourceCase":"22.3","sourceLecture":27,"part2":true,"kind":"DDx","title":"Original source case Part 2 · 22.3","source":"Cases_Part2.docx · Case 22.3 · approved source","signal":"11 y MN domestic shorthair","history":"PU/PD and polyphagia for 6 weeks, lost 1 kg; no steroids, no IV fluids; urine collected by cystocentesis","exam":"BCS 3/9, dull coat, about 5% dehydrated, T 38.5 C","panels":[{"name":"Selected source results · full report below","rows":[["Glucose (mg/dL)","468","71-148"],["Fructosamine","520","190-365"],["BUN (mg/dL)","31","16-36"],["HCT (%)","36","30-45"]]},{"name":"Additional selected source results","rows":[["Sediment","many rods","none"]]}],"smear":"stained cytospin of sediment: rods and degenerate neutrophils; 3-5 RBC per HPF","photos":["Part 2 source image placeholder — see original case instructions"],"questions":[],"differentials":[{"name":"Diabetes mellitus","tier":"best","support":"unmanaged diabetes mellitus with early ketosis and a UTI. USG 1.034 is borderline because marked glucosuria overestimates USG; osmotic diuresis, normal BUN and creatinine = no evidence of renal failure","against":"Use the original source comparison rather than assuming the diagnosis from one result.","test":"Review the source document's rule-in/rule-out clues and recommended next tests."},{"name":"Stress hyperglycemia","tier":"possible","support":"See approved source evidence comparison (revealed after submission).","against":"Use the original source comparison rather than assuming the diagnosis from one result.","test":"Review the source document's rule-in/rule-out clues and recommended next tests."},{"name":"Renal glucosuria","tier":"possible","support":"See approved source evidence comparison (revealed after submission).","against":"Use the original source comparison rather than assuming the diagnosis from one result.","test":"Review the source document's rule-in/rule-out clues and recommended next tests."},{"name":"Concurrent urinary tract infection","tier":"unlikely","support":"See approved source evidence comparison (revealed after submission).","against":"Use the original source comparison rather than assuming the diagnosis from one result.","test":"Review the source document's rule-in/rule-out clues and recommended next tests."},{"name":"Diabetic ketosis","tier":"unlikely","support":"See approved source evidence comparison (revealed after submission).","against":"Use the original source comparison rather than assuming the diagnosis from one result.","test":"Review the source document's rule-in/rule-out clues and recommended next tests."}],"originalReport":"Case 22.3 · DDx · Cat\nSource: Urinalysis 2026, glucosuria (transient vs persistent), ketones, bacteriuria and pyuria,\nUSG, collection method (slides 7, 10, 12, 19, 20, 25, 29, 30); patient data invented\nSignalment / History: 11 y MN domestic shorthair; PU/PD and polyphagia for 6 weeks, lost 1\nkg; no steroids, no IV fluids; urine collected by cystocentesis\nExam: BCS 3/9, dull coat, about 5% dehydrated, T 38.5 C\nLab data\n\nTest                Patient              Reference            Flag\nGlucose (mg/dL)     468                  71-148               ↑\nFructosamine        520                  190-365              ↑\n(µmol/L)\n\nTest                Patient              Reference            Flag\nBUN (mg/dL)         31                   16-36\nCreatinine          1.4                  0.8-2.1\n(mg/dL)\nHCT (%)             36                   30-45\nUrine specific      1.034                ≥1.035 (cat,         ↓\ngravity                                  adequate)\n(refractometer)\nUrine glucose       4+                   negative             ↑\n(dipstick)\nUrine ketones       1+                   negative             ↑\n(dipstick)\nUrine pH            6.0                  6.0-7.5\nUrine protein,      1+, neg, neg         neg to trace\nblood, bilirubin\nSediment WBC        15-20                <5                   ↑\n(per HPF)\nSediment            many rods            none                 ↑\nbacteria\n\nCytology / smear: stained cytospin of sediment: rods and degenerate neutrophils; 3-5 RBC per\nHPF\n📷bacteria\n    Photo to add: urine glucose / ketone test-strip result (Urinalysis 2026 slides 19-20) and\n          with neutrophils on a stained cytospin (slide 30)\nYour task: List the differentials for the glucosuria (transient vs persistent) and what you\nwould add for the ketones and the sediment, before you scroll.\nMy differentials:","originalReview":"Answer\n\nDifferential                             Rule in / rule out clue\nDiabetes mellitus (persistent            Glucose 468 is above the cat renal\nglucosuria)                              threshold (290 mg/dL),\n                                         fructosamine up, PU/PD, polyphagia,\n                                         weight loss\nStress hyperglycemia / excitement        Unlikely: weeks of signs and raised\n(transient glucosuria)                   fructosamine (Added); does not\n                                         explain weight loss\nCorticosteroid or iatrogenic dextrose    History negative\n(transient)\nRenal glucosuria (Fanconi, tubular       Ruled out: glucosuria here is with\n\nDifferential                           Rule in / rule out clue\nnecrosis or ischemia)                  hyperglycemia, creatinine normal\nDiabetic ketosis                       Ketones 1+, but the strip detects only\n                                       acetoacetate and acetone, not β-\n                                       hydroxybutyrate (the most common\n                                       ketone); measure BHB (Added)\nSecondary UTI                          Pyuria + many rods on a\n                                       cystocentesis sample, confirmed on a\n                                       stained slide; urine culture (Added)\n  •   Final interpretation: unmanaged diabetes mellitus with early ketosis and a UTI.\n      USG 1.034 is borderline because marked glucosuria overestimates USG; osmotic\n      diuresis, normal BUN and creatinine = no evidence of renal failure\n  •    Concept link: glucosuria occurs when blood glucose exceeds the renal threshold (dog\n       180-220, cat 290, horse/cattle 150 mg/dL). Strip false negatives for glucose: ascorbic\n       acid, ketones, very concentrated or cold urine, bilirubinuria; false positives: bleach,\n       hydrogen peroxide (slide 25)\nMy notes:"},
{"id":"P2-22.4","sourceCase":"22.4","sourceLecture":27,"part2":true,"kind":"SBA","title":"Original source case Part 2 · 22.4","source":"Cases_Part2.docx · Case 22.4 · approved source","signal":"14 y Quarter Horse gelding, clinically normal at a routine visit","history":"owner is worried because the free-catch sample he collected was cloudy and turned red-brown after 2 days in the refrigerator","exam":"normal; no PU/PD, no pigmenturia seen at voiding, no muscle stiffness","panels":[{"name":"Selected source results · full report below","rows":[["HCT (%)","36","32-48"],["CK (U/L)","240","100-350"]]}],"smear":"sediment: abundant large spheres with radial striations (calcium carbonate), moderate mucus, no casts","photos":["Part 2 source image placeholder — see original case instructions"],"questions":[{"q":"Which interpretation is best?","options":["Hematuria from urolithiasis; schedule surgery","Myoglobinuria from exertional rhabdomyolysis","Physiologic findings of normal adult equine urine plus a storage change","Hemoglobinuria from intravascular hemolysis","Inadequate concentrating ability because USG is below the cat cutoff of 1.035"],"answer":2,"why":"Answer: C adult equine urine is naturally cloudy (mucus, calcium carbonate crystals), pH 7.5- 8.5 is normal for herbivores, and prolonged refrigeration turns horse urine red-brown (pyrocatechin) = physiologic, not pathologic."}],"differentials":[],"originalReport":"Case 22.4 · SBA · Horse\nSource: Urinalysis 2026, species differences: color, clarity, pH, calcium carbonate, reagent\nartifacts (slides 8, 9, 11, 14, 19, 25, 37); patient data invented\nSignalment / History: 14 y Quarter Horse gelding, clinically normal at a routine visit; owner is\nworried because the free-catch sample he collected was cloudy and turned red-brown after 2\ndays in the refrigerator\nExam: normal; no PU/PD, no pigmenturia seen at voiding, no muscle stiffness\nLab data\n\nTest                Patient              Reference            Flag\nHCT (%)             36                   32-48\nCreatinine          1.4                  1.0-1.8\n(mg/dL)\nCK (U/L)            240                  100-350\nUrine specific      1.032                ≥1.025 (horse,\ngravity                                  adequate)\n(refractometer)\nUrine pH            8.5                  7.5-8.5\nUrine protein       1+                   neg to trace         ↑\n(dipstick)\nUrine blood,        all negative         negative\nglucose, ketones,\n\nTest                 Patient              Reference            Flag\nbilirubin\nSediment RBC         0-2                  <5\n(per HPF)\n\nCytology / smear: sediment: abundant large spheres with radial striations (calcium\ncarbonate), moderate mucus, no casts\n📷tablePhoto to add: calcium carbonate crystals (Urinalysis 2026 slide 37) and the urine color\n       (slide 8)\nQuestion: Which interpretation is best?\nA. Hematuria from urolithiasis; schedule surgery\nB. Myoglobinuria from exertional rhabdomyolysis\nC. Physiologic findings of normal adult equine urine plus a storage change\nD. Hemoglobinuria from intravascular hemolysis\nE. Inadequate concentrating ability because USG is below the cat cutoff of 1.035","originalReview":"Answer: C adult equine urine is naturally cloudy (mucus, calcium carbonate crystals), pH 7.5-\n8.5 is normal for herbivores, and prolonged refrigeration turns horse urine red-brown\n(pyrocatechin) = physiologic, not pathologic.\n  •    A: heme is negative and RBC are 0-2/HPF, so no hematuria; crystalluria is a risk factor\n       but not a reliable indicator of urolithiasis\n  •    B: myoglobin would give a positive heme pad and a markedly high CK; CK is normal\n  •    D: hemoglobin would also give a positive heme pad and the HCT is normal\n  •    E: the cutoffs are species specific (dog ≥1.030, cat ≥1.035, horse and cattle ≥1.025); 1.032\n       is adequate for a horse\nConcept link: calcium carbonate crystals are normal in horses, rabbits, guinea pigs and goats\n(alkaline pH), never in dogs or cats. Herbivores (horse, cattle) have urine pH 7.5-8.5 vs 6.0-7.5\nin dog and cat, and a renal glucose threshold of 150 mg/dL. Added: the protein pad reads\nfalsely positive at pH >8.0 (slide 25), so confirm the 1+ with USG, sediment and UPC\nMy notes:"},
{"id":"P2-22.5","sourceCase":"22.5","sourceLecture":27,"part2":true,"kind":"DDx","title":"Original source case Part 2 · 22.5","source":"Cases_Part2.docx · Case 22.5 · approved source","signal":"5 y FS Cocker Spaniel","history":"lethargic and weak for 3 days, owner reports dark red-brown urine; no toxin exposure","exam":"pale and icteric mucous membranes, HR 150, T 39.6 C, mild splenomegaly, no muscle pain","panels":[{"name":"Selected source results · full report below","rows":[["HCT (%)","17","37-55"],["Hemoglobin","5.9","12-18"],["RBC (x10^6/µL)","2.30","5.5-8.5"],["MCV (fL)","74","60-77"],["MCHC (g/dL)","34.7","32-36"],["Absolute","276","<80"],["CK (U/L)","140","50-400"]]}],"smear":"spherocytes, polychromasia, autoagglutination; plasma pink and icteric; after centrifugation the urine supernatant stays red-brown (small pellet)","photos":["Part 2 source image placeholder — see original case instructions"],"questions":[],"differentials":[{"name":"Hemoglobinuria from intravascular hemolysis","tier":"best","support":"regenerative hemolytic anemia (suspect IMHA) with hemoglobinuria and bilirubinuria; brown granular (pigment) casts = tubular injury, no azotemia yet, USG still adequate. Next: slide agglutination test and Coombs test (Added), monitor BUN and creatinine","against":"Use the original source comparison rather than assuming the diagnosis from one result.","test":"Review the source document's rule-in/rule-out clues and recommended next tests."},{"name":"Hematuria","tier":"possible","support":"See approved source evidence comparison (revealed after submission).","against":"Use the original source comparison rather than assuming the diagnosis from one result.","test":"Review the source document's rule-in/rule-out clues and recommended next tests."},{"name":"Myoglobinuria","tier":"possible","support":"See approved source evidence comparison (revealed after submission).","against":"Use the original source comparison rather than assuming the diagnosis from one result.","test":"Review the source document's rule-in/rule-out clues and recommended next tests."},{"name":"Methemoglobinuria","tier":"unlikely","support":"See approved source evidence comparison (revealed after submission).","against":"Use the original source comparison rather than assuming the diagnosis from one result.","test":"Review the source document's rule-in/rule-out clues and recommended next tests."},{"name":"Bilirubinuria","tier":"unlikely","support":"See approved source evidence comparison (revealed after submission).","against":"Use the original source comparison rather than assuming the diagnosis from one result.","test":"Review the source document's rule-in/rule-out clues and recommended next tests."}],"originalReport":"Case 22.5 · DDx · Dog\nSource: Urinalysis 2026, urine color, heme forms (hemoglobin / myoglobin / methemoglobin),\nhematuria, bilirubinuria, casts, reagent artifacts (slides 8, 21, 22, 23, 25, 28, 33); patient data\ninvented\nSignalment / History: 5 y FS Cocker Spaniel; lethargic and weak for 3 days, owner reports\ndark red-brown urine; no toxin exposure\n\nExam: pale and icteric mucous membranes, HR 150, T 39.6 C, mild splenomegaly, no muscle\npain\nLab data\n\nTest                Patient              Reference           Flag\nHCT (%)             17                   37-55               ↓\nHemoglobin          5.9                  12-18               ↓\n(g/dL)\nRBC (x10^6/µL)      2.30                 5.5-8.5             ↓\nMCV (fL)            74                   60-77\nMCHC (g/dL)         34.7                 32-36\nAbsolute            276                  <80                 ↑\nreticulocytes\n(x10^3/µL)\nTotal bilirubin     6.2                  0-0.3               ↑\n(mg/dL)\nCK (U/L)            140                  50-400\nBUN (mg/dL) /       24 / 1.0             8-28 / 0.5-1.4\ncreatinine\n(mg/dL)\nUrine specific      1.034                ≥1.030\ngravity\nUrine blood         3+                   negative            ↑\n(dipstick)\nUrine bilirubin     3+                   neg to 1+ in        ↑\n(dipstick)                               concentrated dog\n                                         urine\nUrine protein,      1+, neg, neg         neg to 1+\nglucose, ketones\nSediment RBC        0-2                  <5\n(per HPF)\nSediment casts      2-3 brown            <2 per LPF,         ↑\n                    granular casts       hyaline\n                    per LPF\n\nCytology / smear: spherocytes, polychromasia, autoagglutination; plasma pink and icteric;\nafter centrifugation the urine supernatant stays red-brown (small pellet)\n📷andPhoto  to add: centrifuged urine tubes, hematuria vs pigmenturia (Urinalysis 2026 slide 22)\n     the urine color table (slide 8); blood smear with spherocytes and agglutination (no slide in\nthis lecture; use the regenerative anemia lecture image)\n\nYour task: Name the pigment (or pigments) behind the dark urine and what separates each\nfrom the others, before you scroll.\nMy differentials:","originalReview":"Answer\n\nDifferential                           Rule in / rule out clue\nHemoglobinuria (intravascular             Heme 3+ with only 0-2 RBC,\nhemolysis, likely IMHA)                   supernatant stays red-brown after\n                                          spinning, pink plasma, HCT 17,\n                                          spherocytes, autoagglutination\nHematuria                                 Ruled out: RBC would spin down as\n                                          a red pellet with a yellow\n                                          supernatant; sediment has 0-2 RBC\nMyoglobinuria                             Unlikely: CK is expected to be high\n                                          with muscle breakdown; CK 140, no\n                                          muscle pain, plasma pink (Added:\n                                          myoglobin clears from plasma\n                                          quickly)\nMethemoglobinuria (oxidized Hgb,          Unlikely: urine is red-brown, no\nbrown-black)                              oxidant exposure; confirm with blood\n                                          color (Added)\nBilirubinuria (conjugated bilirubin, Bilirubin 3+ with T bili 6.2 and\nyellow-orange)                            icterus; 1+ is normal in concentrated\n                                          dog urine but 3+ is significant\nStrip artifact                            Heme false positives: bleach,\n                                          microbial or leukocyte peroxidase;\n                                          false negatives: ascorbic acid, high\n                                          USG, captopril, formaldehyde.\n                                          Bilirubin false positives: indican,\n                                          etodolac metabolites; false negative:\n                                          ascorbic acid, and UV light converts\n                                          bilirubin to biliverdin (not detected)\n   •    Final interpretation: regenerative hemolytic anemia (suspect IMHA) with\n        hemoglobinuria and bilirubinuria; brown granular (pigment) casts = tubular injury, no\n        azotemia yet, USG still adequate. Next: slide agglutination test and Coombs test\n        (Added), monitor BUN and creatinine\n  •    Concept link: the strip detects 3 heme forms (hemoglobin, methemoglobin, myoglobin)\n       and lyses intact RBC on the pad, so use sediment and centrifugation to tell which one;\n       the strip detects mainly conjugated bilirubin (slide 23)\nMy notes:"},
{"id":"P2-22.6","sourceCase":"22.6","sourceLecture":27,"part2":true,"kind":"SBA","title":"Original source case Part 2 · 22.6","source":"Cases_Part2.docx · Case 22.6 · approved source","signal":"2 y MN domestic shorthair","history":"vomiting, ataxia and lethargy; antifreeze spill found in the garage about 18 h ago; urine by cystocentesis (small volume)","exam":"depressed, 7% dehydrated, painful kidneys on palpation, T 37.6 C","panels":[{"name":"Selected source results · full report below","rows":[["BUN (mg/dL)","88","16-36"],["Calcium (mg/dL)","8.4","8.8-11.0"],["Na / K (mmol/L)","150 / 5.9","147-156 / 3.5-5.1"],["Cl (mmol/L)","114","112-122"],["HCO3 (mmol/L)","11","17-24"]]},{"name":"Additional selected source results","rows":[["Glucose (mg/dL)","110","70-150"]]}],"smear":"sediment: many colorless hemp-seed / picket-fence crystals (birefringent), a few envelope-shaped crystals, 3-5 granular casts per LPF, a few tubular epithelial cells","photos":["Part 2 source image placeholder — see original case instructions"],"questions":[{"q":"Which interpretation of the crystalluria fits this patient best?","options":["Struvite (\"coffin lid\") crystals; common in normal cats, no work-up needed","Calcium oxalate monohydrate crystals from ethylene glycol toxicosis with acute kidney","Ammonium biurate crystals from a portosystemic shunt","Calcium carbonate crystals, a normal finding","Bilirubin crystals from cholestasis"],"answer":1,"why":"Answer: B hemp-seed / picket-fence crystals at an acidic to neutral pH, a high anion gap acidosis (KLUE: ethylene glycol), an increased osmolal gap, azotemia, hyperphosphatemia and hypocalcemia fit ethylene glycol toxicosis."}],"differentials":[],"originalReport":"Case 22.6 · SBA · Cat\nSource: Urinalysis 2026, crystals and casts, glucosuria with tubular injury (slides 19, 33, 34, 35-\n45); Electrolytes slides 41-43 (KLUE, osmolal gap); patient data invented\nSignalment / History: 2 y MN domestic shorthair; vomiting, ataxia and lethargy; antifreeze\nspill found in the garage about 18 h ago; urine by cystocentesis (small volume)\nExam: depressed, 7% dehydrated, painful kidneys on palpation, T 37.6 C\nLab data\n\nTest                 Patient              Reference            Flag\nBUN (mg/dL)          88                   16-36                ↑\nCreatinine           5.8                  0.8-2.1              ↑\n(mg/dL)\nPhosphorus           11.2                 3.0-6.0              ↑\n(mg/dL)\nCalcium (mg/dL)      8.4                  8.8-11.0             ↓\nNa / K (mmol/L)      150 / 5.9            147-156 / 3.5-5.1    K↑\nCl (mmol/L)          114                  112-122\nHCO3 (mmol/L)        11                   17-24                ↓\nAnion gap            30.9                 12-25                ↑\n(mmol/L)\nGlucose (mg/dL)      110                  70-150\nCalculated           349                  290-310              ↑\nosmolality\n(mOsm/kg)\nMeasured             385                  290-310              ↑\nosmolality\n(mOsm/kg)\nOsmolal gap          36                   <10                  ↑\n(measured minus\ncalculated)\nUrine specific       1.012                ≥1.035 (cat)         ↓\ngravity\nUrine pH             6.0                  6.0-7.5\nUrine glucose        2+                   negative             ↑\n(dipstick)\nUrine protein,       2+, 1+               neg to trace         ↑\nblood\n\nCytology / smear: sediment: many colorless hemp-seed / picket-fence crystals\n(birefringent), a few envelope-shaped crystals, 3-5 granular casts per LPF, a few tubular\nepithelial cells\n📷41) and\n     Photo to add: calcium oxalate monohydrate \"picket fence\" crystals (Urinalysis 2026 slide\n         calcium oxalate dihydrate \"envelopes\" (slide 40)\nQuestion: Which interpretation of the crystalluria fits this patient best?\nA. Struvite (\"coffin lid\") crystals; common in normal cats, no work-up needed\nB. Calcium oxalate monohydrate crystals from ethylene glycol toxicosis with acute kidney\ninjury\nC. Ammonium biurate crystals from a portosystemic shunt\nD. Calcium carbonate crystals, a normal finding\nE. Bilirubin crystals from cholestasis","originalReview":"Answer: B hemp-seed / picket-fence crystals at an acidic to neutral pH, a high anion gap\nacidosis (KLUE: ethylene glycol), an increased osmolal gap, azotemia, hyperphosphatemia\nand hypocalcemia fit ethylene glycol toxicosis.\n  •    A: struvite is 3-D \"coffin lids\" in neutral to alkaline urine and would not explain the\n       azotemia or the gap\n  •    C: ammonium biurate is brown \"thorn apple\" spheres; seen with portosystemic shunts\n       or hepatic dysfunction\n  •    D: calcium carbonate crystals are large striated spheres, normal in horses, rabbits,\n       guinea pigs and goats, not in cats\n  •    E: bilirubin crystals are yellow needles; abnormal in non-dog species and point to\n       cholestasis, but there is no icterus or hepatic disease here\nConcept link: crystalluria is a risk factor for urolithiasis, not a reliable indicator, and not every\nethylene glycol case has CaOx monohydrate crystals. Glucosuria with normal blood glucose (110,\ncat threshold 290) = tubular injury. Isosthenuria (1.012) = tubules cannot concentrate or dilute.\nCats with urinary obstruction usually have dried blood and organic matter plugs, not crystals\n(slide 34). Added: reference intervals for the anion gap and osmolal gap are typical values\nMy notes:"},
{"id":"P2-23.1","sourceCase":"23.1","sourceLecture":30,"part2":true,"kind":"DDx","title":"Original source case Part 2 · 23.1","source":"Cases_Part2.docx · Case 23.1 · approved source","signal":"3 y MN Akita presented for extraction of a fractured tooth","history":"healthy, no drugs. The pre-anesthetic sample sat on the clot for 3 h before the tube was spun; serum is hemolyzed (2+) and lipemic (4+)","exam":"normal apart from the tooth, HR 88, normal ECG, no collapse, no vomiting or diarrhea","panels":[{"name":"Selected source results · full report below","rows":[["Na (mmol/L)","128","145-152"],["K (mmol/L)","7.4","3.6-5.3"],["Cl (mmol/L)","93","107-115"],["HCO3 (mmol/L)","23","18-25"],["Na:K ratio","17.3","26.8-39.6"]]},{"name":"Additional selected source results","rows":[["Glucose (mg/dL)","95","65-118"],["CK (U/L)","140","50-400"]]}],"smear":"not applicable (chemistry only); platelet estimate adequate, no thrombocytosis","photos":["Part 2 source image placeholder — see original case instructions"],"questions":[],"differentials":[{"name":"Pseudohyperkalemia from hemolysis/delayed separation","tier":"best","support":"spurious Na, Cl and K from a lipemic, hemolyzed, delayed sample in a high-RBC-K breed. Recollect, spin and separate promptly, and run Na and K by direct ISE (blood gas analyzer) before any ACTH stimulation test (Added)","against":"Use the original source comparison rather than assuming the diagnosis from one result.","test":"Review the source document's rule-in/rule-out clues and recommended next tests."},{"name":"Pseudohyponatremia/pseudohypochloremia from lipemia","tier":"possible","support":"See approved source evidence comparison (revealed after submission).","against":"Use the original source comparison rather than assuming the diagnosis from one result.","test":"Review the source document's rule-in/rule-out clues and recommended next tests."},{"name":"Hypoadrenocorticism","tier":"possible","support":"See approved source evidence comparison (revealed after submission).","against":"Use the original source comparison rather than assuming the diagnosis from one result.","test":"Review the source document's rule-in/rule-out clues and recommended next tests."},{"name":"Uroabdomen or obstruction","tier":"unlikely","support":"See approved source evidence comparison (revealed after submission).","against":"Use the original source comparison rather than assuming the diagnosis from one result.","test":"Review the source document's rule-in/rule-out clues and recommended next tests."},{"name":"EDTA contamination","tier":"unlikely","support":"See approved source evidence comparison (revealed after submission).","against":"Use the original source comparison rather than assuming the diagnosis from one result.","test":"Review the source document's rule-in/rule-out clues and recommended next tests."}],"originalReport":"Case 23.1 · DDx · Dog\nSource: Electrolytes slides 19, 20, 22, 27, 34-36 (lipemia artifact, pseudohyperkalemia in Akita,\nNa:K ratio, corrected chloride) and practice case slide 26 (healthy dog with hyperkalemia);\npatient data invented\nSignalment / History: 3 y MN Akita presented for extraction of a fractured tooth; healthy, no\ndrugs. The pre-anesthetic sample sat on the clot for 3 h before the tube was spun; serum is\nhemolyzed (2+) and lipemic (4+)\nExam: normal apart from the tooth, HR 88, normal ECG, no collapse, no vomiting or diarrhea\nLab data\n\nTest                Patient              Reference            Flag\nNa (mmol/L)         128                  145-152              ↓\nK (mmol/L)          7.4                  3.6-5.3              ↑\nCl (mmol/L)         93                   107-115              ↓\nHCO3 (mmol/L)       23                   18-25\nAnion gap           19.4                 16.7-25.5\n(mmol/L)\nNa:K ratio          17.3                 26.8-39.6            ↓\nCorrected Cl        108                  107-115\n(mmol/L)\nCalculated          282                  290-310              ↓\nosmolality\n(mOsm/kg)\nMeasured            296                  290-310\nosmolality\n\nTest                Patient              Reference            Flag\n(mOsm/kg)\nGlucose (mg/dL)     95                   65-118\nBUN (mg/dL) /       17 / 0.9             11-27 / 0.5-1.4\ncreatinine\n(mg/dL)\nCalcium             10.2 / 4.4           9.5-11.1 / 2.6-5.3\n(mg/dL) /\nphosphorus\n(mg/dL)\nCK (U/L)            140                  50-400\nPlatelets           310                  150-500\n(x10^3/µL)\n\nCytology / smear: not applicable (chemistry only); platelet estimate adequate, no\nthrombocytosis\n📷lipemia\n     Photo to add: serum tubes showing lipemia and hemolysis (no slide; use a hemolysis /\n         index chart) and the pseudohyperkalemia list (Electrolytes slide 22)\nYour task: List the differentials for the abnormal Na, Cl and K and say which are artifacts,\nbefore you scroll.\nMy differentials:","originalReview":"Answer\n\nDifferential                             Rule in / rule out clue\nPseudohyperkalemia (hemolysis,           Akita has high RBC K (also Shiba\ndelayed separation)                      Inu, horses, pigs, most cattle);\n                                         hemolysis 2+, 3 h on the clot; healthy,\n                                         normal ECG, normal BUN, creatinine,\n                                         HCO3\nPseudohyponatremia and                   Lipemia 4+; Na and Cl fall together\npseudohypochloremia (lipemia,            (corrected Cl 108 is normal);\nindirect potentiometry)                  calculated osmolality is lower than\n                                         the measured one because Na is\n                                         falsely low (Added: osmolal gap 14)\nHypoadrenocorticism (Addison's)          Na:K 17.3 looks like it (<19), but no\n                                         signs, normal glucose, BUN,\n                                         creatinine and Ca; the ratio comes\n                                         from two artifacts\nUroabdomen / urinary obstruction         No azotemia, no abdominal fluid\nMetabolic acidosis (K shift out of       HCO3 23 and anion gap normal\ncells)\n\nDifferential                              Rule in / rule out clue\nCell lysis (rhabdomyolysis, tumor         CK and phosphorus normal\nlysis)\nEDTA contamination                      Ca 10.2 is normal (EDTA gives high K\n                                        and low Ca)\nExtreme thrombocytosis                  Platelets 310, not high\n  •   Final interpretation: spurious Na, Cl and K from a lipemic, hemolyzed, delayed sample\n      in a high-RBC-K breed. Recollect, spin and separate promptly, and run Na and K by\n      direct ISE (blood gas analyzer) before any ACTH stimulation test (Added)\n  •    Concept link: pseudohyperkalemia = thrombocytosis, hemolysis in high-RBC-K species,\n       EDTA (use a red or green top); lipemia lowers Na and Cl together; Na:K <27 or <19 is\n       only useful with trustworthy values\nMy notes:"},
{"id":"P2-23.2","sourceCase":"23.2","sourceLecture":30,"part2":true,"kind":"SBA","title":"Original source case Part 2 · 23.2","source":"Cases_Part2.docx · Case 23.2 · approved source","signal":"5-day-old Holstein calf","history":"profuse diarrhea for 2 days, now weak and not suckling","exam":"recumbent, sunken eyes, severe dehydration","panels":[{"name":"Selected source results · full report below","rows":[["PCV (%)","56","26-44"],["Glucose (mg/dL)","31","45-75"],["BUN (mg/dL)","64","20-30"],["Na (mmol/L)","122","132-152"]]},{"name":"Additional selected source results","rows":[["K (mmol/L)","6.7","3.9-5.8"],["Cl (mmol/L)","86","97-111"],["TCO2 (mmol/L)","11","17-29"]]}],"smear":"not applicable (chemistry only)","photos":["Part 2 source image placeholder — see original case instructions"],"questions":[{"q":"Which interpretation of the acid-base status and the hyperkalemia is best?","options":["Secretional (non-gap) metabolic acidosis from fecal bicarbonate loss; K is high from tissue","Titrational (high anion gap) metabolic acidosis with Cl following Na; K is high from acidemia","Metabolic alkalosis from abomasal sequestration; K is high from cell lysis","Pseudohyponatremia and pseudohypochloremia from lipemia; K is high from hemolysis","No acid-base disorder; all changes are from dehydration"],"answer":1,"why":"Answer: B TCO2 11 with a gap of 31.7 and a corrected Cl of 100 (normal) = gap acidosis: L- lactate from hypovolemia, D-lactate from bacterial fermentation in calf scours, and uremic acids (BUN 64, creatinine 3.3). The K shift out of cells with acidemia plus hypovolemia (low tubular flow) raises K."}],"differentials":[],"originalReport":"Case 23.2 · SBA · Calf\nSource: adapted from lecture practice case, 5-day-old Holstein calf (Electrolytes slides 19, 22,\n34-36, 41-43, 51); exam findings invented\nSignalment / History: 5-day-old Holstein calf; profuse diarrhea for 2 days, now weak and not\nsuckling\nExam: recumbent, sunken eyes, severe dehydration\nLab data\n\nTest                 Patient              Reference            Flag\nPCV (%)              56                   26-44                ↑\nGlucose (mg/dL)      31                   45-75                ↓\nBUN (mg/dL)          64                   20-30                ↑\nCreatinine           3.3                  1-2                  ↑\n(mg/dL)\nNa (mmol/L)          122                  132-152              ↓\nK (mmol/L)           6.7                  3.9-5.8              ↑\nCl (mmol/L)          86                   97-111               ↓\nTCO2 (mmol/L)        11                   17-29                ↓\nAnion gap            31.7                 6-14                 ↑\n(mmol/L)\nCorrected Cl         100                  97-111\n(mmol/L)\n\nCytology / smear: not applicable (chemistry only)\n\n📷(slidePhoto\n         51)\n             to add: the KLUE graphic (Electrolytes slide 43) or the calf table with red values\n\n\nQuestion: Which interpretation of the acid-base status and the hyperkalemia is best?\nA. Secretional (non-gap) metabolic acidosis from fecal bicarbonate loss; K is high from tissue\ndamage\nB. Titrational (high anion gap) metabolic acidosis with Cl following Na; K is high from acidemia\nshift and low renal excretion\nC. Metabolic alkalosis from abomasal sequestration; K is high from cell lysis\nD. Pseudohyponatremia and pseudohypochloremia from lipemia; K is high from hemolysis\nE. No acid-base disorder; all changes are from dehydration","originalReview":"Answer: B TCO2 11 with a gap of 31.7 and a corrected Cl of 100 (normal) = gap acidosis: L-\nlactate from hypovolemia, D-lactate from bacterial fermentation in calf scours, and uremic\nacids (BUN 64, creatinine 3.3). The K shift out of cells with acidemia plus hypovolemia (low\ntubular flow) raises K.\n  •    A: non-gap acidosis would have a high Cl and a normal anion gap\n  •    C: alkalosis would have a high HCO3 and a selectively low Cl\n  •    D: lipemia does not lower TCO2 or raise the gap; check hemolysis anyway, because most\n       cattle have high RBC K (Added)\n  •    E: dehydration explains the PCV, BUN and creatinine, but not a TCO2 of 11 with a gap of\n       31.7\nConcept link: Na and Cl both low = Na lost in diarrhea in excess of water (Cl follows Na);\nKLUE = Ketones, Lactate, Uremic acids, Ethylene glycol raise the gap. Hypoglycemia (31) is\ncommon in septic or diarrheic neonates (Added)\nMy notes:"},
{"id":"P2-23.3","sourceCase":"23.3","sourceLecture":30,"part2":true,"kind":"DDx","title":"Original source case Part 2 · 23.3","source":"Cases_Part2.docx · Case 23.3 · approved source","signal":"4 mo FI Labrador","history":"lethargic and whimpering. Earlier today a neighbor found and returned the puppy after she had gotten out; before that she was a normal energetic puppy","exam":"HR 70, weak femoral pulses, abdominal distension with a fluid wave, painful abdomen, no diarrhea","panels":[{"name":"Selected source results · full report below","rows":[["BUN (mg/dL)","42","11-27"],["Calcium (mg/dL)","11.2","9.5-11.1"],["Albumin (g/dL)","3.7","3.2-4.3"],["Na (mmol/L)","144","145-152"]]},{"name":"Additional selected source results","rows":[["K (mmol/L)","6.8","3.6-5.3"],["Cl (mmol/L)","106","107-115"],["HCO3 (mmol/L)","20","18-25"],["Na:K ratio","21.2","26.8-39.6"]]}],"smear":"not applicable (chemistry only)","photos":["Part 2 source image placeholder — see original case instructions"],"questions":[],"differentials":[{"name":"Uroabdomen","tier":"best","support":"low Na:K from hyponatremia + hyperkalemia, most likely uroabdomen. Acid-base: HCO3 and anion gap are normal and corrected Cl is normal (Cl follows Na), so no acid-base disorder; \"abnormally normal\" gap despite azotemia is worth noting","against":"Use the original source comparison rather than assuming the diagnosis from one result.","test":"Review the source document's rule-in/rule-out clues and recommended next tests."},{"name":"Hypoadrenocorticism","tier":"possible","support":"See approved source evidence comparison (revealed after submission).","against":"Use the original source comparison rather than assuming the diagnosis from one result.","test":"Review the source document's rule-in/rule-out clues and recommended next tests."},{"name":"Oliguric renal disease","tier":"possible","support":"See approved source evidence comparison (revealed after submission).","against":"Use the original source comparison rather than assuming the diagnosis from one result.","test":"Review the source document's rule-in/rule-out clues and recommended next tests."},{"name":"Gastroenteritis","tier":"unlikely","support":"See approved source evidence comparison (revealed after submission).","against":"Use the original source comparison rather than assuming the diagnosis from one result.","test":"Review the source document's rule-in/rule-out clues and recommended next tests."},{"name":"Repeated chylothorax drainage","tier":"unlikely","support":"See approved source evidence comparison (revealed after submission).","against":"Use the original source comparison rather than assuming the diagnosis from one result.","test":"Review the source document's rule-in/rule-out clues and recommended next tests."}],"originalReport":"Case 23.3 · DDx · Dog\nSource: adapted from lecture practice case, 4 mo FI Labrador (Electrolytes slides 27, 34-36, 52-\n54); exam findings invented\nSignalment / History: 4 mo FI Labrador; lethargic and whimpering. Earlier today a neighbor\nfound and returned the puppy after she had gotten out; before that she was a normal\nenergetic puppy\nExam: HR 70, weak femoral pulses, abdominal distension with a fluid wave, painful abdomen,\nno diarrhea\nLab data\n\nTest                Patient             Reference            Flag\nBUN (mg/dL)         42                  11-27                ↑\nCreatinine          2.5                 0.5-1.4              ↑\n(mg/dL)\nPhosphorus          10.1                2.6-5.3              ↑\n(mg/dL)\nCalcium (mg/dL)     11.2                9.5-11.1             ↑\nAlbumin (g/dL)      3.7                 3.2-4.3\nNa (mmol/L)         144                 145-152              ↓\nK (mmol/L)          6.8                 3.6-5.3              ↑\nCl (mmol/L)         106                 107-115              ↓\nHCO3 (mmol/L)       20                  18-25\nAnion gap           24.8                16.7-25.5\n(mmol/L)\nNa:K ratio          21.2                26.8-39.6            ↓\nCorrected Cl        109                 107-115\n(mmol/L)\n\nCytology / smear: not applicable (chemistry only)\n📷Na:KPhoto to add: the puppy table with red abnormal values (Electrolytes slides 52-54) and the\n      ratio list (slide 27)\nYour task: Why is the Na:K ratio low? List the differentials from the lecture and pick the one\nthe history favors, before you scroll.\nMy differentials:","originalReview":"Answer\n\nDifferential                            Rule in / rule out clue\nUroabdomen (ruptured bladder or         Escaped and was found, abdominal\nurethra after trauma)                   distension with fluid; azotemia,\n                                        hyperkalemia, hyperphosphatemia,\n                                        low Na and Cl as abdominal fluid\n                                        equilibrates; abdominal fluid\n                                        creatinine and K higher than serum\n                                        (Added)\nHypoadrenocorticism (Addison's)         Na:K 21.2 is <27 (suspicious) but\n                                        not <19; acute onset in a puppy with\n                                        a trauma history is against it; ACTH\n                                        stimulation test if uroabdomen is\n                                        ruled out (Added)\nRenal disease (oliguric / anuric)       Azotemia, hyperK,\n\nDifferential                             Rule in / rule out clue\n                                         hyperphosphatemia fit, but anion gap\n                                         is normal (uremic acids would raise\n                                         it); check urine USG and ultrasound\n                                         (Added)\nGastroenteritis (e.g. whipworm)          No vomiting or diarrhea\nRepeated drainage of a chylothorax       No effusion drainage history\n  •    Final interpretation: low Na:K from hyponatremia + hyperkalemia, most likely\n       uroabdomen. Acid-base: HCO3 and anion gap are normal and corrected Cl is normal\n       (Cl follows Na), so no acid-base disorder; \"abnormally normal\" gap despite azotemia is\n       worth noting\n  •    Concept link: causes of a decreased Na:K ratio = renal disease, Addison's,\n       gastroenteritis, uroabdomen, repeated drainage of chylothorax; corrected Cl = Cl x (Na\n       normal midpoint / Na measured) = 106 x 148.5 / 144 = 109\nMy notes:"},
{"id":"P2-23.4","sourceCase":"23.4","sourceLecture":30,"part2":true,"kind":"SBA","title":"Original source case Part 2 · 23.4","source":"Cases_Part2.docx · Case 23.4 · approved source","signal":"grower pigs, several \"acting weird\"","history":"water lines froze yesterday afternoon and were fixed by evening so the pigs could drink again","exam":"some pigs press their head against a wall or corner; some sit like dogs, jerk the head back and fall to the side","panels":[{"name":"Selected source results · full report below","rows":[["Na (mmol/L)","172","135-150"],["Cl (mmol/L)","126","94-106"],["K (mmol/L)","5.2","4.4-6.5"],["HCO3 (mmol/L)","26","18-28"]]},{"name":"Additional selected source results","rows":[["BUN (mg/dL)","38","10-30"],["Glucose (mg/dL)","88","70-120"]]}],"smear":"not applicable (chemistry only)","photos":["Part 2 source image placeholder — see original case instructions"],"questions":[{"q":"Which interpretation and treatment caution is best?","options":["Selective hyperchloremia (secretional metabolic acidosis); give IV sodium bicarbonate","Sodium bicarbonate toxicosis; Na is high but Cl should be low","Water-deficit hypernatremia with Cl following Na; rehydrate gradually","Primary salt excess only; give unlimited free water now","Central diabetes insipidus; give ADH and free water now"],"answer":2,"why":"Answer: C Na and Cl are both high and corrected Cl is normal (Cl moves with Na) after a period of water deprivation = water deficit (“salt poisoning” in the lecture's sense of water deprivation). Na >170 explains the neurologic signs (brain cells shrink); rapid correction can cause cerebral edema."}],"differentials":[],"originalReport":"Case 23.4 · SBA · Pig\nSource: adapted from lecture example, grower pigs and the frozen water lines (Electrolytes\nslides 15-18, 20); lab values invented\nSignalment / History: grower pigs, several \"acting weird\"; water lines froze yesterday\nafternoon and were fixed by evening so the pigs could drink again\nExam: some pigs press their head against a wall or corner; some sit like dogs, jerk the head\nback and fall to the side\nLab data\n\nTest                Patient              Reference           Flag\nNa (mmol/L)         172                  135-150             ↑\nCl (mmol/L)         126                  94-106              ↑\nK (mmol/L)          5.2                  4.4-6.5\nHCO3 (mmol/L)       26                   18-28\nCorrected Cl        104                  94-106\n(mmol/L)\nBUN (mg/dL)         38                   10-30               ↑\nGlucose (mg/dL)     88                   70-120\nCalculated          373                  290-310             ↑\nosmolality\n(mOsm/kg)\nMeasured            379                  290-310             ↑\nosmolality\n\nTest                 Patient              Reference            Flag\n(mOsm/kg)\n\nCytology / smear: not applicable (chemistry only)\n📷(slidePhoto\n         15)\n             to add: the grower pig photos (Electrolytes slides 16 and 18) and the Na disorder list\n\n\nQuestion: Which interpretation and treatment caution is best?\nA. Selective hyperchloremia (secretional metabolic acidosis); give IV sodium bicarbonate\nB. Sodium bicarbonate toxicosis; Na is high but Cl should be low\nC. Water-deficit hypernatremia with Cl following Na; rehydrate gradually\nD. Primary salt excess only; give unlimited free water now\nE. Central diabetes insipidus; give ADH and free water now","originalReview":"Answer: C Na and Cl are both high and corrected Cl is normal (Cl moves with Na) after a\nperiod of water deprivation = water deficit (“salt poisoning” in the lecture's sense of water\ndeprivation). Na >170 explains the neurologic signs (brain cells shrink); rapid correction can\ncause cerebral edema.\n  •    A: corrected Cl 104 is normal and HCO3 is normal, so Cl is not selectively high\n  •    B: bicarbonate gives Na excess without Cl and a high HCO3; here Cl is high and HCO3 is\n       normal\n  •    D: sudden rehydration with hypotonic water risks cell swelling, brain edema and death\n  •    E: the history (frozen lines) explains the water deficit; DI is not needed\nConcept link: hypernatremia = water deficit (deprivation, DI, osmotic diuresis, panting, GI\nloss) or Na excess (salt poisoning with Cl, NaHCO3 without Cl). Calculated osmolality = 2(Na +\nK) + glucose/18 + BUN/2.8 = 373. Added: correcting over 24-48 h is a common\nrecommendation\nMy notes:"},
{"id":"P2-23.5","sourceCase":"23.5","sourceLecture":30,"part2":true,"kind":"DDx","title":"Original source case Part 2 · 23.5","source":"Cases_Part2.docx · Case 23.5 · approved source","signal":"5 y Holstein cow, 10 days fresh","history":"off feed for 2 days, milk production down, scant pasty feces","exam":"HR 96, about 6% dehydrated, \"ping\" over the left last rib, no fever","panels":[{"name":"Selected source results · full report below","rows":[["Na (mmol/L)","140","132-152"],["K (mmol/L)","3.3","3.9-5.8"],["Cl (mmol/L)","80","97-111"],["TCO2 (mmol/L)","50","17-29"]]},{"name":"Additional selected source results","rows":[["BUN (mg/dL)","34","20-30"]]}],"smear":"not applicable (chemistry only)","photos":["Part 2 source image placeholder — see original case instructions"],"questions":[],"differentials":[{"name":"Left displaced abomasum","tier":"best","support":"metabolic alkalosis (high TCO2, corrected Cl low = Cl moves separately from Na, anion gap normal). Low K from alkalosis shift and poor intake. Paradoxical aciduria: hypovolemia activates RAAS, Na is resorbed but Cl is scarce, so H+ and K+ are excreted instead, giving acidic urine and worsening the alkalosis and hypokalemia (the herbivore urine pH should be 7.5-8.5)","against":"Use the original source comparison rather than assuming the diagnosis from one result.","test":"Review the source document's rule-in/rule-out clues and recommended next tests."},{"name":"Right displaced abomasum or volvulus","tier":"possible","support":"See approved source evidence comparison (revealed after submission).","against":"Use the original source comparison rather than assuming the diagnosis from one result.","test":"Review the source document's rule-in/rule-out clues and recommended next tests."},{"name":"Pyloric obstruction","tier":"possible","support":"See approved source evidence comparison (revealed after submission).","against":"Use the original source comparison rather than assuming the diagnosis from one result.","test":"Review the source document's rule-in/rule-out clues and recommended next tests."},{"name":"Diuretic-associated chloride loss","tier":"unlikely","support":"See approved source evidence comparison (revealed after submission).","against":"Use the original source comparison rather than assuming the diagnosis from one result.","test":"Review the source document's rule-in/rule-out clues and recommended next tests."},{"name":"Bovine renal failure","tier":"unlikely","support":"See approved source evidence comparison (revealed after submission).","against":"Use the original source comparison rather than assuming the diagnosis from one result.","test":"Review the source document's rule-in/rule-out clues and recommended next tests."}],"originalReport":"Case 23.5 · DDx · Cow\nSource: Electrolytes slides 40, 46-49 (hypochloremia, urine pH, paradoxical aciduria, displaced\nabomasum); Urinalysis 2026 slide 14 (herbivore urine pH 7.5-8.5); patient data invented\nSignalment / History: 5 y Holstein cow, 10 days fresh; off feed for 2 days, milk production\ndown, scant pasty feces\nExam: HR 96, about 6% dehydrated, \"ping\" over the left last rib, no fever\nLab data\n\nTest                Patient              Reference            Flag\nNa (mmol/L)         140                  132-152\nK (mmol/L)          3.3                  3.9-5.8              ↓\nCl (mmol/L)         80                   97-111               ↓\nTCO2 (mmol/L)       50                   17-29                ↑\nAnion gap           13.3                 6-14\n(mmol/L)\nCorrected Cl        81                   97-111               ↓\n(mmol/L)\nBUN (mg/dL)         34                   20-30                ↑\nCreatinine          1.8                  1-2\n(mg/dL)\nUrine pH            5.5                  7.5-8.5 (cattle)     ↓\n\nCytology / smear: not applicable (chemistry only)\n📷with-Na\n    Photo to add: the paradoxical aciduria flow chart (Electrolytes slides 47-48) and the Cl-\n         interpretation chart (slides 37-38)\nYour task: Characterize the acid-base status, name the likely cause and explain the acidic\nurine, before you scroll.\nMy differentials:","originalReview":"Answer\n\nDifferential                             Rule in / rule out clue\nLeft displaced abomasum (upper GI Left-sided ping, fresh cow, off feed;\nsequestration of HCl)                    selective low Cl with high HCO3\nRight displaced abomasum / volvulus Possible (also sequesters HCl and is\n                                         usually more severe); the ping is on\n                                         the left here\nPyloric obstruction                      Possible upper GI sequestration; less\n                                         likely without chronic signs (Added)\nHemorrhagic bowel syndrome in            Unlikely: scant pasty feces, no melena\ncows                                     or colic described (Added)\nDiuretic (furosemide)                    No treatment history\nBovine renal failure                     Unlikely: creatinine 1.8 is normal\n                                         (BUN rise is pre-renal)\n  •     Final interpretation: metabolic alkalosis (high TCO2, corrected Cl low = Cl moves\n        separately from Na, anion gap normal). Low K from alkalosis shift and poor intake.\n        Paradoxical aciduria: hypovolemia activates RAAS, Na is resorbed but Cl is scarce, so\n        H+ and K+ are excreted instead, giving acidic urine and worsening the alkalosis and\n        hypokalemia (the herbivore urine pH should be 7.5-8.5)\n\n  •    Concept link: metabolic alkalosis pattern = high HCO3, low Cl, normal anion gap;\n       paradoxical aciduria needs hypovolemia + hypochloremia + hypokalemia. Added:\n       treatment is Cl- and K-containing fluids, because correcting the alkalosis alone can\n       worsen hypokalemia\nMy notes:"},
{"id":"P2-27.6","sourceCase":"27.6","sourceLecture":35,"part2":true,"kind":"SBA","title":"Original source case Part 2 · 27.6","source":"Cases_Part2.docx · Case 27.6 · approved source","signal":"22 y Morgan mare","history":"long curly coat that never shed, weight loss with a pot belly, PU/PD, lethargy, recurrent hoof abscesses; sampled in September","exam":"generalized hypertrichosis, topline muscle atrophy, rounded abdomen, HR 38 bpm","panels":[{"name":"Additional selected source results","rows":[["Insulin (µIU/mL) 38","<20","↑"]]}],"smear":"not applicable","photos":["Part 2 source image placeholder — see original case instructions"],"questions":[{"q":"Which statement about this mare's disease is correct?","options":["Functional adrenal cortical tumor with hypercortisolemia; a dexamethasone suppression test","Pars intermedia adenoma after loss of periventricular dopaminergic neurons; adrenals are","Conn's syndrome; measure aldosterone and renin","The same disease as canine PDH (anterior pituitary corticotroph adenoma); use an ACTH","A baseline ACTH of 125 pg/mL means the same in January and September"],"answer":1,"why":"Answer: B - PPID is a pars intermedia adenoma whose melanotrophs overproduce POMC, αMSH, β-endorphin, CLIP and ACTH that tends not to be bioactive (slide 34)."}],"differentials":[],"originalReport":"Case 27.6 · SBA · Horse\nSource: lecture slides 34-39 (equine PPID); patient data invented\nNote: teaching points cite slides 8, 30-39. Added: ACTH cutoffs are approximate values read off\nthe slide 39 graph; the insulin interval is typical.\nSignalment / History: 22 y Morgan mare; long curly coat that never shed, weight loss with a\npot belly, PU/PD, lethargy, recurrent hoof abscesses; sampled in September\nExam: generalized hypertrichosis, topline muscle atrophy, rounded abdomen, HR 38 bpm\nLab data\n\nTest                Patient             Reference            Flag\nBaseline ACTH,   125                    unlikely <27;        ↑\nSeptember                               supportive >88\n(pg/mL)\nInsulin (µIU/mL) 38                     <20                  ↑\nGlucose (mg/dL) 112                     70-120\n\nCytology / smear: not applicable\n📷slidePhoto\n       39)\n            to add: horse with hypertrichosis (Adrenal slide 35); seasonal ACTH graph (Adrenal\n\n\nQuestion: Which statement about this mare's disease is correct?\nA. Functional adrenal cortical tumor with hypercortisolemia; a dexamethasone suppression test\nis the test of choice\nB. Pars intermedia adenoma after loss of periventricular dopaminergic neurons; adrenals are\nnormal, hypercortisolemia is not typical, and baseline ACTH is read against season-specific\ncutoffs\nC. Conn's syndrome; measure aldosterone and renin\nD. The same disease as canine PDH (anterior pituitary corticotroph adenoma); use an ACTH\nstim test\nE. A baseline ACTH of 125 pg/mL means the same in January and September","originalReview":"Answer: B - PPID is a pars intermedia adenoma whose melanotrophs overproduce POMC,\nαMSH, β-endorphin, CLIP and ACTH that tends not to be bioactive (slide 34).\n  •    A: adrenals are morphologically normal and hypercortisolemia is not typical, so \"equine\n       Cushing's\" fell out of favor (slide 34).\n  •    C: Conn's is aldosterone excess with hypokalemia and ventroflexion (slides 30-32).\n  •    D: canine PDH is anterior pituitary (slide 8); PPID is the pars intermedia (slide 34).\n  •    E: ACTH is seasonal in equids, peaking in late summer to fall, so cutoffs vary by month\n       (slide 39).\nConcept link: hypertrichosis is strongly suggestive (slide 35); test with baseline ACTH and/or\nTRH stimulation in an unstressed horse, EDTA plasma kept cool (slides 37-38). Disease of older\nequids, generally >15 y (slide 34).\nMy notes:"},
{"id":"P2-29.7","sourceCase":"29.7","sourceLecture":29,"part2":true,"kind":"DDx","title":"Original source case Part 2 · 29.7","source":"Cases_Part2.docx · Case 29.7 · approved source","signal":"3 y MC Labrador, hunting dog","history":"swam in standing water 2 weeks ago; vomiting, lethargy, PU/PD 4 days, dark urine","exam":"temp 39.9 C, icterus, petechiae, tender kidneys and cranial abdomen, 5% dehydrated","panels":[{"name":"Selected source results · full report below","rows":[["HCT (%)","41","37-55"],["WBC (x10^3/uL)","26.4","5.5-16.9"],["BUN (mg/dL)","96","7-27"],["Na (mEq/L)","138","144-153"],["K (mEq/L)","3.3","3.6-5.3"],["Cl (mEq/L)","100","106-116"]]},{"name":"Additional selected source results","rows":[["HCO3 (mEq/L)","16","17-25"],["Glucose (mg/dL)","95","75-120"],["ALT (U/L)","480","10-100"],["ALP (U/L)","620","20-150"],["GGT (U/L)","18","0-7"],["Albumin (g/dL)","2.4","2.6-3.3"],["Urine SG","1.012",">1.030"],["Urine casts","granular 3-5 per","<2 hyaline"]]}],"smear":"neutrophilia with left shift, mild toxicity; thrombocytopenia","photos":["Part 2 source image placeholder — see original case instructions"],"questions":[],"differentials":[{"name":"Leptospirosis","tier":"best","support":"Leptospirosis-type pattern: renal azotemia (BUN 96, creatinine 4.9, hyperphosphatemia, USG 1.012), tubular injury (glucosuria with normoglycemia, granular casts), hepatocellular and cholestatic injury, thrombocytopenia from vasculitis, systemic inflammatory leukogram. Low K and Na with vomiting and polyuria fits a non-oliguric pattern. Confirm with MAT and PCR of blood or urine; Added: it is zoonotic.","against":"Use the original source comparison rather than assuming the diagnosis from one result.","test":"Review the source document's rule-in/rule-out clues and recommended next tests."},{"name":"Ethylene glycol exposure","tier":"possible","support":"See approved source evidence comparison (revealed after submission).","against":"Use the original source comparison rather than assuming the diagnosis from one result.","test":"Review the source document's rule-in/rule-out clues and recommended next tests."},{"name":"Ischemic or NSAID-associated acute kidney injury","tier":"possible","support":"See approved source evidence comparison (revealed after submission).","against":"Use the original source comparison rather than assuming the diagnosis from one result.","test":"Review the source document's rule-in/rule-out clues and recommended next tests."},{"name":"Hepatobiliary disease with hepatorenal injury","tier":"unlikely","support":"See approved source evidence comparison (revealed after submission).","against":"Use the original source comparison rather than assuming the diagnosis from one result.","test":"Review the source document's rule-in/rule-out clues and recommended next tests."},{"name":"Tick-borne disease","tier":"unlikely","support":"See approved source evidence comparison (revealed after submission).","against":"Use the original source comparison rather than assuming the diagnosis from one result.","test":"Review the source document's rule-in/rule-out clues and recommended next tests."}],"originalReport":"Case 29.7 · DDx · Dog\nSource: Integrates renal, urinalysis, hepatobiliary, hemostasis and electrolytes lectures; patient\ndata invented\nSignalment / History: 3 y MC Labrador, hunting dog; swam in standing water 2 weeks ago;\nvomiting, lethargy, PU/PD 4 days, dark urine\nExam: temp 39.9 C, icterus, petechiae, tender kidneys and cranial abdomen, 5% dehydrated\nLab data\n\nTest                Patient              Reference            Flag\nHCT (%)             41                   37-55\nWBC (x10^3/uL)      26.4                 5.5-16.9             ↑\nNeutrophils         22.2                 3.0-11.5             ↑\n(x10^3/uL)\nBands               1.6                  0-0.3                ↑\n(x10^3/uL)\nPlatelets           62                   200-500              ↓\n(x10^3/uL)\nBUN (mg/dL)         96                   7-27                 ↑\nCreatinine          4.9                  0.5-1.5              ↑\n(mg/dL)\nPhosphorus          9.4                  2.5-6.8              ↑\n(mg/dL)\nNa (mEq/L)          138                  144-153              ↓\nK (mEq/L)           3.3                  3.6-5.3              ↓\n\nTest                Patient              Reference            Flag\nCl (mEq/L)          100                  106-116              ↓\nHCO3 (mEq/L)        16                   17-25                ↓\nGlucose (mg/dL)     95                   75-120\nTotal bilirubin     6.8                  0-0.3                ↑\n(mg/dL)\nALT (U/L)           480                  10-100               ↑\nALP (U/L)           620                  20-150               ↑\nGGT (U/L)           18                   0-7                  ↑\nAlbumin (g/dL)      2.4                  2.6-3.3              ↓\nUrine SG            1.012                >1.030               ↓\nUrine glucose       2+                   negative             ↑\nUrine bilirubin /   3+ / 2+              1+ can be            ↑\nprotein                                  normal /\n                                         negative\nUrine casts         granular 3-5 per     <2 hyaline           ↑\n                    lpf\n\nAdded: reference intervals are typical values, not from the slides. Corrected Cl = 100 x 148.5 /\n138 = 107.6 (normal), so the low Cl follows the low Na. AG = (138 + 3.3) - (100 + 16) = 25.3.\nCytology / smear: neutrophilia with left shift, mild toxicity; thrombocytopenia\n📷urinalysis\n    Photo to add: urine sediment with granular casts; no slide number known, pull from the\n            lecture\nYour task: List your top 3-5 differentials for AKI + hepatopathy + thrombocytopenia and the\nnext test for each before you scroll.\nMy differentials:","originalReview":"Answer\n\nDifferential                             Rule in / rule out clue\nLeptospirosis (most likely)              Water exposure, fever, AKI with\n                                         isosthenuria + glucosuria with\n                                         normal blood glucose (tubular\n                                         damage), hepatocellular and\n                                         cholestatic changes,\n                                         thrombocytopenia, neutrophilia;\n                                         MAT and PCR\nEthylene glycol                          AKI with high AG acidosis and CaOx\n                                         monohydrate crystals; no liver\n                                         involvement\nIschemic or NSAID AKI                    No icterus or liver enzyme rise of this\n\nDifferential                              Rule in / rule out clue\n                                          size\nHepatobiliary disease with                Liver would dominate; renal azotemia\nhepatorenal injury                        with isosthenuria is less typical\nTick-borne disease (Ehrlichia,            Thrombocytopenia, but no marked\nAnaplasma)                                AKI or jaundice\n  •    Final interpretation: Leptospirosis-type pattern: renal azotemia (BUN 96, creatinine\n       4.9, hyperphosphatemia, USG 1.012), tubular injury (glucosuria with normoglycemia,\n       granular casts), hepatocellular and cholestatic injury, thrombocytopenia from\n       vasculitis, systemic inflammatory leukogram. Low K and Na with vomiting and polyuria\n       fits a non-oliguric pattern. Confirm with MAT and PCR of blood or urine; Added: it is\n       zoonotic.\n  •    Concept link: isosthenuria + azotemia = renal; persistent glucosuria with normal blood\n       glucose = tubular dysfunction or necrosis, not DM; 3+ bilirubinuria is abnormal (only\n       1+ is normal, and only in concentrated dog urine).\nMy notes:"},
{"id":"P2-29.8","sourceCase":"29.8","sourceLecture":39,"part2":true,"kind":"SBA","title":"Original source case Part 2 · 29.8","source":"Cases_Part2.docx · Case 29.8 · approved source","signal":"14 y FS DSH (3.8 kg)","history":"lost 1.4 kg over 6 months despite polyphagia, PU/PD, restless, vomits occasionally","exam":"HR 240, palpable thyroid nodule, systolic BP 190 mmHg, BCS 3/9 with muscle wasting","panels":[{"name":"Selected source results · full report below","rows":[["HCT (%)","46","30-45"],["RBC (x10^6/uL)","8.85","5.0-10.0"],["MCV (fL)","52","39-50"],["Hb (g/dL)","15.1","9.5-15.0"],["MCHC (g/dL)","32.8","31-35"],["ALT (U/L)","220","25-97"],["ALP (U/L)","138","14-71"]]},{"name":"Additional selected source results","rows":[["BUN (mg/dL)","38","18-33"],["SDMA (ug/dL)","21","0-14"],["Urine SG","1.022",">1.035"],["Total T4 (ug/dL)","9.8","0.8-2.3 (gray zone"],["Free T4 (ng/dL)","6.2","0.8-2.0"]]}],"smear":"some Heinz bodies, otherwise unremarkable","photos":["Part 2 source image placeholder — see original case instructions"],"questions":[{"q":"What is the best interpretation of these results?","options":["Hyperthyroidism only; kidneys are normal because creatinine is within the reference","Hyperthyroidism with concurrent CKD masked by the thyroid effect; BUN, SDMA and USG","Primary CKD with secondary hyperthyroidism","Diabetes mellitus (polyuria, polyphagia and weight loss)","Euthyroid sick syndrome"],"answer":1,"why":"Answer: B - tT4 and fT4 are both high (hyperthyroid); creatinine falls from muscle wasting and GFR rises, hiding CKD, while up BUN, up SDMA and USG 1.022 point to impaired kidneys."}],"differentials":[],"originalReport":"Case 29.8 · SBA · Cat\nSource: Integrates thyroid/adrenal, renal, hemogram and urinalysis lectures (tT4\ninterpretation from the endocrine case set); patient data invented\nSignalment / History: 14 y FS DSH (3.8 kg); lost 1.4 kg over 6 months despite polyphagia,\nPU/PD, restless, vomits occasionally\nExam: HR 240, palpable thyroid nodule, systolic BP 190 mmHg, BCS 3/9 with muscle wasting\nLab data\n\nTest                Patient            Reference           Flag\nHCT (%)             46                 30-45               ↑\nRBC (x10^6/uL)      8.85               5.0-10.0\nMCV (fL)            52                 39-50               ↑\nHb (g/dL)           15.1               9.5-15.0            ↑\nMCHC (g/dL)         32.8               31-35\nALT (U/L)           220                25-97               ↑\nALP (U/L)           138                14-71               ↑\nCreatinine          1.9                0.8-2.1\n(mg/dL)\nBUN (mg/dL)         38                 18-33               ↑\nSDMA (ug/dL)        21                 0-14                ↑\nPhosphorus          5.4                3.0-6.0\n(mg/dL)\n\nTest                Patient               Reference            Flag\nUrine SG            1.022                 >1.035               ↓\nTotal T4 (ug/dL)    9.8                   0.8-2.3 (gray zone   ↑\n                                          2.4-4.7)\nFree T4 (ng/dL)     6.2                   0.8-2.0              ↑\n\nAdded: reference intervals are typical values, not from the slides except tT4. Check: 8.85 x 52 /\n10 = 46; MCHC 15.1/46 = 32.8. Creatinine stays \"normal\" because of muscle wasting and\nincreased GFR.\nCytology / smear: some Heinz bodies, otherwise unremarkable\n📷endocrine\n    Photo to add: cat with a palpable thyroid nodule, or the thyroid case slide from the\n           case set\nYour task: Pick the single best answer.\nQuestion: What is the best interpretation of these results?\nA. Hyperthyroidism only; kidneys are normal because creatinine is within the reference\ninterval\nB. Hyperthyroidism with concurrent CKD masked by the thyroid effect; BUN, SDMA and USG\nsuggest reduced renal function that will be unmasked after treatment\nC. Primary CKD with secondary hyperthyroidism\nD. Diabetes mellitus (polyuria, polyphagia and weight loss)\nE. Euthyroid sick syndrome","originalReview":"Answer: B - tT4 and fT4 are both high (hyperthyroid); creatinine falls from muscle wasting\nand GFR rises, hiding CKD, while up BUN, up SDMA and USG 1.022 point to impaired kidneys.\n  •    A: creatinine is unreliable here; SDMA, BUN and USG show renal compromise (15-49%\n       of hyperthyroid cats become azotemic after treatment).\n  •    C: CKD does not cause hyperthyroidism; tT4 is far above the gray zone.\n  •    D: no hyperglycemia or glucosuria; polyphagia fits hyperthyroidism.\n  •    E: NTIS lowers tT4, it does not raise it.\nConcept link: hyperthyroid cats show erythrocytosis, macrocytosis, Heinz bodies, up ALT and\nALP and low creatinine; assess kidneys with SDMA, USG and BUN before and after treatment.\nAdded: concurrent illness can suppress tT4 into the gray zone, so run fT4.\nMy notes:"}
